// src/v4/std/collection.dag
//
// Scope: container carriers grounded in algebras from std/algebra.dag —
//        the container-side operations are reads of those algebras,
//        never an enumerated per-op table (the NO-ENGINE STOP per
//        std/algebra.dag header U1). The two carriers have DIFFERENT
//        cardinality stories — read them honestly:
//          - List<T> = FreeMonoid<T>: a structurally finite sequence;
//            its cardinality (length) IS intrinsic to the carrier,
//            read by walking the Empty|Cons spine to bottom.
//          - Set<T> = characteristic function fn(T) -> Bool: a
//            subset-of-T carrier inhabiting BooleanAlgebra<Set<T>>. It
//            does NOT structurally encode finiteness — the universe
//            and complement constructions in the BooleanAlgebra grounding
//            are deliberately representable (BooleanAlgebra requires
//            them), so a `Set<Int>` such as "all positive ints" is a
//            valid value. Finite-cardinality is a REFINEMENT layer —
//            tracked as deferred (Wave-A2, see scaffold note below)
//            and out of scope for the carrier in this PR.
//        This honest split was surfaced by the codex BLOCKING review
//        (#3169) flagging an earlier "finite subsets / count `true`
//        elements" over-claim on Set; the carrier is unchanged, the
//        framing here is tightened to match it (P1/P4/Practice-2).
// Anchor: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Free_monoid (List) +
//         https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Algebra_of_sets (Set)
//
// Owns:
//   - List<T> — finite sequences (the free-monoid carrier over T;
//     finiteness is STRUCTURAL — every value bottoms out at Empty)
//   - Set<T>  — subset-of-T carrier (characteristic function;
//     inhabits BooleanAlgebra<Set<T>>; finite-cardinality refinement
//     is NOT in this carrier, see "Deferred to Wave-A2" below)
//   - Map<K, V> — partial function K → 1+V with fail-closed lookup
//     (`lookup: fn(K) -> Witness<V>`; operator-ratified Option A,
//     #3169; Wave-A2 / this PR).
//   - operations defined as algebra-grounding, not enumerated
//
// Deferred (split out per operator-ratified Option A, 2026-05-16 —
// see "Deferred to Wave-A2" scaffold note below):
//   - FiniteSet<T> (or Set<T, n: Multiplicity>) — gated on the
//     cardinality / enumerability substrate landing; the bare Set<T>
//     here is the unrefined BooleanAlgebra carrier, by design.
//   - Machine-readable BooleanAlgebra<Set<T>> inhabitance edge —
//     gated on parametric `data` instances OR a pipeline-level
//     structural-grounding-reader landing; the BooleanAlgebra
//     grounding for Set<T> lives in prose today (pointwise-lift
//     derivation), not as a substrate-readable edge.
//
// Consumes:
//   - std/node.dag: Cardinality, Instantiation — the type-substrate
//     connectives the kernel uses to represent parameterized container
//     types in the type DAG. (No Cardinality TYPE is consumed; the
//     refinement-type carrier `Multiplicity` lives in std/cardinality.dag
//     — UNMERGED at #3163 — and is NOT consumed by collection.dag
//     today per the operator-ratified b2 decision.)
//   - std/algebra.dag: FreeMonoid<T> (the recursive carrier List
//     grounds in by carrier identity). BooleanAlgebra<Set<T>> grounds
//     Set by pointwise lift but appears here only as a header
//     reference, not as a parametric data instance (see modeling
//     notes — there is no v2 parametric-instance form).
//   - std/witness.dag: Witness<V> (Map.lookup codomain — operator
//     Option A partial-function discipline, #3169).
//   (Bool and the type parameters T, K, V are kernel-ambient; Symbol
//   is substrate-ambient per std/node.dag K-1 — none is imported.)
//
// Header reconciliation — OPERATOR-RATIFIED b2, 2026-05-16:
//   The prior scaffold header named `List<T, n: Cardinality>,
//   Set<T, n>, Map<K, V, n>` with `Cardinality` from node.dag — but
//   `Cardinality` in node.dag is a Connective VARIANT, not a usable
//   refinement type, and the real refinement type lives in
//   std/cardinality.dag as `Multiplicity` (unmerged #3163). The
//   contract as originally written was unbuildable. The operator
//   ratified option b2 (plain carriers — for List<T> = FreeMonoid<T>,
//   cardinality (length) IS intrinsic via the FreeMonoid spine; for
//   Set<T> as a characteristic function fn(T) -> Bool, finite-
//   cardinality is NOT intrinsic to the carrier and is a deferred
//   refinement — see scaffold item (2) below) + option A (Map<K,V>
//   split to Wave-A2, gated on witness.dag); this header is the
//   reconciled b2 form. The original frozen-header STOP write-up
//   lives in #3169 issuecomment-4464775997.
//
// Deferred to Wave-A2 — TRACKED SCAFFOLD (🟡):
//
// (1) `Map<K, V>` — DISSOLVED 2026-05-16 (PR #3187). The carrier
//   `Map<K, V> { lookup: fn(K) -> Witness<V> }` is declared below;
//   `std/witness.dag` (T-3) is merged. Duplicate keys remain
//   structurally unrepresentable (single total function K → Witness).
//   The prior Wave-A1 scaffold text for item (1) is retired here.
//
// (2) `FiniteSet<T>` (or `Set<T, n: Multiplicity>`) — a
//   finite-cardinality refinement of the bare Set<T> below. Codex
//   BLOCKING review (#3169, 2026-05-16) correctly observed that
//   `Set<T> = { member: fn(T) -> Bool }` denotes ARBITRARY subsets
//   (including universe / complement-of-finite for infinite T) and
//   does not encode finiteness or witness `T`'s enumerability;
//   counting `true` elements requires an enumeration of T that the
//   carrier does not carry. The honest move (this PR): keep the bare
//   Set<T> as the BooleanAlgebra carrier (universe + complement must
//   be representable for BooleanAlgebra closure — they are not
//   "illegal states" against THIS carrier's spec), and DEFER the
//   finite-cardinality refinement. Three bridge properties:
//     (a) scaffold doc — THIS note; finite-cardinality is not
//         claimed by Set<T> below.
//     (b) bounds on use — pipeline stages / lenses needing a finite
//         subset wait on the refinement; bare Set<T> is for the
//         membership-as-predicate use case, not the bounded-finite
//         one.
//     (c) dissolution trigger — when the cardinality / enumerability
//         substrate lands (std/cardinality.dag's `Multiplicity` +
//         a `Witness`-typed enumerability witness on T, both Wave-A2),
//         a follow-up PR adds a `FiniteSet<T>` carrier that pairs
//         `member` with that witness; bare Set<T> remains as the
//         arbitrary-subset BooleanAlgebra carrier. (Tracking the
//         same operator-ratified deferral pattern as Map.)
//
// (3) `BooleanAlgebra<Set<T>>` inhabitance — PROSE-ONLY grounding,
//   pending a machine-readable substrate edge. Codex BLOCKING review
//   on #3169 (sha bc10dfb3) correctly observed that Set<T>'s
//   BooleanAlgebra grounding (the pointwise-lift section in the
//   Set<T> docstring below) lives only in COMMENTS — not as a
//   substrate-readable inhabitance edge a downstream pipeline /
//   lens consumer can mechanically traverse. This is genuinely an
//   expressiveness gap, NOT a one-off omission:
//     - List<T> = FreeMonoid<T> grounds by TYPE ALIAS — the
//       inhabitance IS machine-readable via the alias edge (the
//       type IS the FreeMonoid carrier).
//     - Set<T> = { member: fn(T) -> Bool } is NOT an alias to
//       BooleanAlgebra's carrier (BooleanAlgebra is a STRUCTURE
//       record, not a carrier); the inhabitance is by POINTWISE
//       LIFT — a derivation, not an alias. No type-alias edge to
//       read, no `data` instance encoded (parametric `data
//       set_T_boolean_algebra<T>: BooleanAlgebra<Set<T>>` has no
//       precedent in the v2 seed; and even if it did, a stored
//       instance would be a second authority for the algebra the
//       carrier already structurally is — the INVARIANTS P2 +
//       algebra.dag U1 "engine debt" the modeling notes below
//       cite for declining the data-instance route).
//   Three bridge properties:
//     (a) scaffold doc — THIS note; the BooleanAlgebra<Set<T>>
//         inhabitance is documented prose under the Set<T> type
//         below, and is NOT machine-readable as a substrate edge.
//     (b) bounds on use — Wave-A1 consumers that need Set's
//         BooleanAlgebra ops compose them from `Bool`'s
//         BooleanAlgebra by pointwise lift at the call site,
//         using the prose-documented constructions; no consumer
//         reads a `BooleanAlgebra<Set<T>>` instance edge today
//         because none exists.
//     (c) dissolution trigger — when EITHER the v2/seed gains a
//         parametric `data` instance form (so we can author
//         `data set_T_boolean_algebra<T>: BooleanAlgebra<Set<T>>`
//         without breaking P2 / engine-debt by also making it the
//         single authority), OR the pipeline / lens framework
//         lands a structural-grounding-reader that mechanically
//         derives BooleanAlgebra instances from pointwise-lift
//         declarations, this note dissolves and the inhabitance
//         becomes machine-readable. Until then the gap is named
//         here, bounded as above, and not silently expanded.
//
// NO ENGINE — NO ENUMERATED OPS. There is deliberately no
// `list_concat` / `set_union` table here: those would be a parallel
// representation of structure std/algebra.dag already owns (the
// `kernel_algebra_profile` debt v3 carried, which std/algebra.dag's
// U1 note explicitly forbids — "not a name-keyed table lookup").
// Consumers read the algebra structures from algebra.dag and the
// carrier shapes here; ops are reads of that one authority, not
// table rows.
//
// Status: T-3 Wave-A1 modeled 2026-05-16 (witty-dove-121); header
// reconciled to operator-ratified b2 + Option A in the same round.
// Wave-A2 Map<K,V> (PR #3187) after witness.dag merge. Splitting this
// file requires explicit operator ratification (substrate extension =
// stop signal).


module v4.std.collection


import v4.std.algebra { FreeMonoid }
import v4.std.witness { Witness }


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Modeling notes (read before validating against the anchor)
//
// CARRIERS, NOT STRUCTURES. This file owns CONTAINER CARRIERS. The
// algebra structures they ground in (Monoid, BooleanAlgebra) are owned
// by std/algebra.dag; the inhabitant owns its OWN grounding here per
// std/algebra.dag's SCOPE note. The grounding is the structural
// alias / pointwise lift — there is no per-carrier `data
// <name>: <Algebra><Carrier> = <Algebra> { … }` instance, for two
// reasons that compose:
//   (i)  v2-data instance-values are not parametric — no precedent
//        for `data list_T_monoid<T>: Monoid<List<T>> = Monoid { … }`
//        in the v2 seed (the closest precedent is concrete-typed
//        `data timing_lens_monoid: Monoid<TimingMeasurement>` in v3
//        timing_lens.dag), and a parametric instance would need a
//        substrate the seed doesn't yet have.
//   (ii) Even if it did, a stored instance would be a SECOND
//        AUTHORITY for the algebra the carrier already IS
//        (INVARIANTS P2 — the std/algebra.dag header U1's "engine
//        debt" — a name-keyed table lookup mirroring the structure
//        it traces).
// The aliasing IS the inhabitance — the same pattern std/text.dag
// uses for `String = FreeMonoid<Char>`.
//
// NO NEW COPRODUCTS. The declarations below are a TYPE ALIAS onto a
// coproduct owned elsewhere (List → FreeMonoid in algebra.dag) and
// two Conj records (Set, Map). No new Disj declared here means no new
// Practice-4 5-pattern ledger applies — the FreeMonoid<T> ledger
// already lives in algebra.dag and is the one authority.
//
// OPS FALL OUT OF GROUNDING — illustrated, not enumerated. The
// header names which algebra each carrier grounds in and how the
// ops project. Authoring those projections as concrete functions
// here would be the per-op table the brief forbids; they are
// pipeline-stage / lens reads of (carrier shape) ⊗ (algebra
// structure), consumed at the use site.
//
// PARTIAL-ACCESS OPS — STOP on Outcome<T>. Any List / Set operation
// that would need the value-or-Diagnostic carrier (Outcome<T>) —
// e.g. an `unsafe_head` returning either a value or a
// "list-was-empty" Diagnostic — is a STOP per the operator-ratified
// pending-carrier substrate (not yet in v4). The CARRIERS themselves
// (List<T>, Set<T>) are Wave-A1-clean; no such op is authored here.
// Consumers needing partial access wait on Outcome<T> landing.
//
// Map<K,V> + Witness<V> (review note, 2026-05-16): `Witness<V>` is
// the shared fail-closed proof carrier from std/witness.dag (lens
// reads use it too). Using it as the codomain of `Map.lookup` is the
// operator-ratified K → 1+V encoding: `Holds` carries the value at
// the key; `Violates` carries a full `Diagnostic` because the witness
// shape requires it — that payload is the **typed absence channel**
// for “no binding at this key”, not a claim that a lens read failed on
// the map artifact. Absence vs read-failure is a **provenance /
// reason** distinction at the producer (P1/P2 single authority for
// the partial arrow; P3 no silent Option::None). A dedicated
// absent-key carrier would be a substrate extension = STOP unless
// ratified.


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// List<T> — finite sequences of T. Structurally identical to
// FreeMonoid<T> (Empty | Cons { head, tail }); the alias IS the
// inhabitance — by being a FreeMonoid<T>, List<T> is a free-monoid
// carrier and inherits the Monoid structure with no parallel data
// instance (std/algebra.dag header: "Concrete sequence types ground
// by aliasing onto it (e.g. a text type onto FreeMonoid over a char
// type)").
//
// Operations (concat, length, fold, map, filter, head, tail) are
// STRUCTURAL READS of the FreeMonoid Empty/Cons constructors composed
// with a Monoid<T> the CALL SITE supplies (the brief's "Monoid for
// fold") — never enumerated here. The element type's algebra is the
// only authority for the per-call reduction; the carrier shape is
// the only authority for the recursion.
//
// Cardinality (length) is INTRINSIC to the carrier — read by walking
// the FreeMonoid spine, not declared as a refinement parameter on
// this type (per the operator-ratified b2 header note).
//
// This declaration is a type ALIAS onto a coproduct owned by
// std/algebra.dag (the FreeMonoid<T> = Empty | Cons ledger lives
// there). No new Practice-4 ledger applies — there is no new
// coproduct to dissolve at this layer.
type List<T> = FreeMonoid<T>


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Set<T> — a subset of T, modeled as a characteristic function. A
// `Set<T>` IS its `member` function: x ∈ S iff `S.member(x) == true`.
// This is the textbook denotational form (Wikipedia "Algebra of
// sets"): a subset is a function T → Bool.
//
// This carrier denotes ARBITRARY subsets, not "finite subsets" —
// `universe` and `complement` below are deliberately representable
// (BooleanAlgebra closure requires them), so a value like "all
// positive ints" is a valid `Set<Int>`. Finite-cardinality is a
// REFINEMENT layer deferred to Wave-A2 (see the "Deferred to
// Wave-A2 — TRACKED SCAFFOLD" block in the file header — codex
// BLOCKING review on #3169 surfaced this distinction). The bare
// Set<T> here is the unrefined BooleanAlgebra carrier; consumers
// needing a guaranteed-finite subset wait on the refinement.
//
// Algebra grounding — Set<T> inhabits BooleanAlgebra<Set<T>> by
// POINTWISE EXTENSION of Bool's BooleanAlgebra:
//
//   union(a, b)              ≡ member: λx. a.member(x) || b.member(x)
//   intersect(a, b)          ≡ member: λx. a.member(x) && b.member(x)
//   complement(a)            ≡ member: λx. ! a.member(x)
//   empty                    ≡ member: λ_. false                (bottom)
//   universe                 ≡ member: λ_. true                 (top)
//   is_subset(a, b)          ≡ ∀x. a.member(x) → b.member(x)
//   difference(a, b)         ≡ intersect(a, complement(b))
//   symmetric_difference     ≡ union(difference(a,b), difference(b,a))
//
// Every one of these falls out of the Bool BooleanAlgebra owned by
// std/algebra.dag composed with this carrier — there is deliberately
// no `data set_T_boolean_algebra: BooleanAlgebra<Set<T>>` instance
// here (the parametric-data + single-authority reasoning in the
// modeling notes). The pointwise lift IS the inhabitance, exactly
// the algebra.dag "aliasing is the inhabitant's concern" pattern —
// BUT note that this grounding lives in PROSE here, not as a
// substrate-readable inhabitance edge a pipeline / lens consumer
// can mechanically traverse; that prose-only-grounding gap is
// itself a tracked scaffold (see "Deferred to Wave-A2 — TRACKED
// SCAFFOLD" block (3) in the file header for bounds + dissolution
// trigger).
//
// Cardinality is NOT intrinsic to Set<T>: counting `true` elements
// requires an enumeration of T, and this carrier does not witness
// `T`'s enumerability. A finite-cardinality refinement
// (`FiniteSet<T>` or `Set<T, n: Multiplicity>`) is deferred to
// Wave-A2 alongside the cardinality / enumerability substrate (the
// header's "Deferred to Wave-A2 — TRACKED SCAFFOLD" block (2) has
// the three bridge properties).
//
// Conj record (one field, `member`); not a coproduct — no Practice-4
// ledger applies.
type Set<T> {
  member: fn(T) -> Bool
}


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Map<K, V> — partial function K → 1+V (operator-ratified Wave-A2 Map
// split, Option A / #3169). Duplicate keys are structurally
// unrepresentable: `lookup` is a total function on K into Witness<V>;
// absence of a value is `Violates{ diagnostic: … }`, not a second key
// binding. See modeling notes above on Witness provenance.
//
// Coproduct dissolution (Practice 4 / INVARIANTS P1 / modeling-
// discipline.md §4) — N/A: Map<K,V> is a Conj record (one field), not
// a sum type — no variant ledger applies.
type Map<K, V> {
  lookup: fn(K) -> Witness<V>
}
